    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n#include <omp.h>\\n#include <float.h>\\n\\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    long total = 0;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) total += groups[i].length;\\n\\n    float *xs = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\\n    float *ys = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\\n    char *labs = malloc(total * sizeof(char));\\n    long idx = 0;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        Group g = groups[i];\\n        for (int j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\\n            xs[idx] = g.points[j].x;\\n            ys[idx] = g.points[j].y;\\n            labs[idx] = g.label;\\n            idx++;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    int nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();\\n    float **tdist = malloc(nthreads * sizeof(float*));\\n    char **tlab = malloc(nthreads * sizeof(char*));\\n    for (int t = 0; t < nthreads; t++) {\\n        tdist[t] = malloc(k * sizeof(float));\\n        tlab[t] = malloc(k * sizeof(char));\\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) tdist[t][i] = -1.0f;\\n    }\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel\\n    {\\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\\n        float *ldist = tdist[tid];\\n        char *llab = tlab[tid];\\n\\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static) nowait\\n        for (long i = 0; i < total; i++) {\\n            float dx = xs[i] - to_evaluate.x;\\n            float dy = ys[i] - to_evaluate.y;\\n            float d = dx * dx + dy * dy;\\n            char lab = labs[i];\\n\\n            int pos = -1;\\n            for (int x = 0; x < k; x++) {\\n                if (ldist[x] == -1.0f || d < ldist[x]) { pos = x; break; }\\n            }\\n            if (pos != -1) {\\n                for (int y = k - 1; y > pos; y--) {\\n                    ldist[y] = ldist[y - 1];\\n                    llab[y] = llab[y - 1];\\n                }\\n                ldist[pos] = d;\\n                llab[pos] = lab;\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    float *gdist = malloc(k * sizeof(float));\\n    char *glab = malloc(k * sizeof(char));\\n    int *ptr = calloc(nthreads, sizeof(int));\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) { gdist[i] = -1.0f; glab[i] = -1; }\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\\n        int best_t = -1;\\n        float best_d = FLT_MAX;\\n        for (int t = 0; t < nthreads; t++) {\\n            if (ptr[t] < k && tdist[t][ptr[t]] != -1.0f) {\\n                float d = tdist[t][ptr[t]];\\n                if (best_t == -1 || d < best_d || (d == best_d && t < best_t)) {\\n                    best_d = d;\\n                    best_t = t;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        if (best_t == -1) break;\\n        gdist[i] = best_d;\\n        glab[i] = tlab[best_t][ptr[best_t]];\\n        ptr[best_t]++;\\n    }\\n\\n    qsort(glab, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\\n\\n    char most_frequent = glab[0];\\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\\n    int current_frequency = 1;\\n\\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\\n        if (glab[i] != glab[i - 1]) {\\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n                most_frequent = glab[i - 1];\\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n            }\\n            current_frequency = 1;\\n        } else {\\n            current_frequency++;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n            most_frequent = glab[i - 1];\\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    for (int t = 0; t < nthreads; t++) { free(tdist[t]); free(tlab[t]); }\\n    free(tdist); free(tlab);\\n    free(xs); free(ys); free(labs);\\n    free(gdist); free(glab); free(ptr);\\n\\n    return most_frequent;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Flatten all points into contiguous arrays and parallelize the distance/top-k loop with OpenMP static work sharing. Each thread maintains a private sorted top-k list using the same sentinel-based insertion as the sequential code; after an implicit barrier, deterministic merging of per-thread lists (tie-breaking by thread id, which corresponds to original point order under contiguous static scheduling) reproduces the exact global k nearest neighbors and preserves output bytes.\",\n  \"notes\": \"Only src/knn.c is modified. The header remains unchanged. OpenMP pragmas are used for work sharing; no changes to input parsing or output format. Per-thread arrays avoid critical-section contention, and the merge step is O(k * nthreads).\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Flattened group/point data into contiguous xs, ys, labs arrays for cache-friendly access.\",\n    \"Added OpenMP parallel-for with static scheduling over all points; each thread maintains a private top-k list using sentinel -1 insertion identical to the sequential algorithm.\",\n    \"Merged per-thread sorted lists deterministically after an implicit barrier, breaking distance ties by thread id (lower tid = earlier original indices under contiguous static chunks).\",\n    \"Reused the existing qsort and frequency-counting logic on the merged label array.\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one HPC source-code experiment. Role C: OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity. We have current sources knn.c and knn.h. Need modify only these files under src/. Must preserve correctness exactly (output bytes). Baseline is sequential ~2.085s. Best observed in interactions: 0.0267s with some source combination; also own history best 0.1089s. The goal is to improve speed using OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling, thread granularity.\n\nWe need propose one candidate transformation. We can modify knn.c and/or knn.h. Need include OpenMP pragmas. Must be careful with correctness: exact output bytes against official reference. The sequential algorithm computes k nearest distances among all points across groups, then sorts labels by char (qsort) and finds most frequent label; tie-breaking? qsort on char ascending, then frequency counting picks first max? Actually it counts runs; if equal frequencies, the earlier in sorted order wins because only updates when current_frequency > most_frequent_count. Since labels are chars sorted ascending, ties go to smaller char (ASCII). Need preserve that.\n\nWe can parallelize distance computation and top-k selection across all points using OpenMP work sharing. Each thread maintains private top-k list; merge deterministically after implicit barrier. This was tried in e1af752126744ea9 with mean 1.665s, not great. Another attempt flattened arrays got 0.16396s (score 0.927). Best interaction 0.0267s likely from some other role? We need propose new.\n\nWe have current source sequential baseline ~2.085s. Need improve significantly. The best observed in interactions is 0.0267s, which is huge speedup (~78x) on 4 cores; maybe using OpenMP reduction and efficient top-k with per-thread arrays and critical merge? We need design.\n\nLet's inspect current source: knn.c uses malloc for labels/distances of size k, loops over groups then points, insertion into sorted array O(k) each