 the one with smaller original index should be kept. Since chunks are contiguous in flattened point order (if we flatten all groups), then global index ordering is preserved across threads: thread t handles indices [start_t, end_t). So when merging, for equal distances, choose candidate from earlier chunk (smaller start) first. We can implement merge by scanning each thread's list with pointers and selecting smallest distance; if tie, select from thread with smaller original index? But we don't store original index in per-thread lists currently. Need store original point index along with label and distance to break ties correctly. The sequential algorithm doesn't need indices because it processes points sequentially and only replaces on strict less; but for parallel merge we need know global order.\n\nAlternative: use OpenMP reduction? Could compute all distances into array, then find k smallest using partial sort? But that would be O(N) memory and maybe slower.\n\nBetter approach: Use per-thread top-k arrays with distance and label plus original index. Then after parallel loop, each thread has sorted list (by distance asc, tie by index asc). Merge them deterministically into global top-k of size k using a priority queue or repeated selection. Since number of threads is small (4), we can do simple merge: for i in 0..k-1, scan all threads' current heads and pick min (distance, then original_index); advance that thread's pointer. This yields exact same as sequential because it respects global order.\n\nNeed flatten groups into contiguous arrays? We can iterate over groups and points; but to assign global indices we need know total count or compute offset per group. Could precompute prefix sums of lengths. Or simpler: use OpenMP parallel for with collapse(2) over groups and points? But variable-length inner loops make static scheduling tricky. Better flatten all points into arrays xs, ys, labels (or just keep Group array but iterate flattened index). We can first compute total_points = sum(g.length), allocate arrays px, py, plabel of size total_points, fill them sequentially. Then parallel for over i=0..total_points-1 with static schedule. This adds O(N) preprocessing but N likely large (baseline 2s suggests many points). Preprocessing is sequential but cheap relative to distance computations.\n\nWe need include OpenMP header and compile flags? The Makefile probably already has -fopenmp? We don't control Makefile, only source files. But the controller compiles with some default flags; likely includes -fopenmp if we use pragmas? In these tasks, they usually expect us to add #pragma omp parallel for and compiler will link OpenMP automatically? The build system may have -fopenmp in CFLAGS already or not. We can't modify Makefile (not allowed). But the instructions say edit only source files under src/. So we must rely on existing compilation flags. In previous experiments, they used OpenMP successfully (e.g., e1af752126744ea9 had mean 1.665s and was valid), so -fopenmp is likely enabled by default or via environment. We'll assume.\n\nNeed ensure correctness: output char only. The function knn returns most_frequent label. Need preserve exact tie-breaking among labels after sorting? qsort on chars ascending, then frequency counting picks first max in sorted order; ties go to smaller ASCII char because it updates only if current_frequency > most_frequent_count (strict). So we must replicate that.\n\nOur parallel top-k selection should produce exactly the same set of k nearest points as sequential. Then sorting labels and counting will be identical. So focus on exact top-k.\n\nLet's design:\n\n1. Compute total_points = sum over groups lengths.\n2. Allocate arrays xs, ys, labs (or just use Group* but flatten).\n3. Fill them sequentially: for each group i, for j in 0..g.length-1, set xs[idx]=g.points[j].x etc., labs[idx]=g.label; idx++.\n4. Parallel region:\n   - Determine number of threads via omp_get_max_threads() or use inside parallel