. So if distances equal, choose candidate from lower tid (since its chunk starts earlier). But what about within a thread's list? It already respects order by index because insertion only on strict less; so for equal distance, the one with smaller index appears first in that thread's list. When merging across threads, we need compare original indices to decide which thread's candidate comes first if distances equal. So store idx along with dist and label.\n\nThus local arrays: float ldist[k]; char llab[k]; int lidx[k]; int cnt (number filled). Initialize all distances to FLT_MAX? But insertion logic can use cnt<k as condition for filling empty slots. We'll implement:\n\n```\nint pos = cnt;\nfor (int x=0; x<cnt; x++) {\n    if (d < ldist[x] || (d == ldist[x] && idx < lidx[x])) { // but within thread, idx increasing so d== and idx< won't happen because later idx > earlier; but safe\n        pos = x;\n        break;\n    }\n}\nif (pos < k) {\n   if (cnt < k) cnt++;\n   for (int y=cnt-1; y>pos; y--) { shift right }\n   ldist[pos]=d; llab[pos]=label; lidx[pos]=idx;\n}\n```\nBut careful: If cnt==k and d is not better than worst, we skip. We can compare with last element.\n\nSimpler: use insertion sort as in sequential but with idx tie-break to be safe across threads? Actually within thread it's unnecessary but harmless. However if two points have same distance and later point has smaller index? Not possible because indices increasing. So condition `d < ldist[x]` suffices for local.\n\nBut when merging, we need global order: For equal distances, choose candidate with smaller original idx. Since static chunks are contiguous in ascending index, thread tid's chunk covers [start_tid, end_tid). If two candidates have same distance from different threads, the one from lower tid has smaller start and thus all its indices < other thread's indices? Not necessarily: if thread0 handles 0..9999, thread1 handles 10000..19999; any index in thread0 < any in thread1. So for equal distance