 of equal-distance points, subsequent equal-distance points won't replace because condition `d < ldist[x]` false for existing equal distances. So lower tid may exclude later equal-distance points even though they have same distance as included ones. That's correct: sequential would also exclude them because it only keeps first k smallest; if there are more than k points with same minimal distance, the earliest indices among those are kept. Since lower tid processes earlier indices, its list will contain the earliest occurrences of that distance within its chunk. Higher tid may have later occurrences but they won't be selected over lower tid's because tie-break by tid (lower wins) and lower tid already has them if it had capacity? But what if lower tid's list is full with distances smaller than d, so it doesn't include any point of distance d; then higher tid might include a point of distance d. In sequential, that point from higher tid would be considered after all points in lower tid (since its index larger). If lower tid had no point of distance d among top-k because there were enough smaller distances, then the first occurrence of distance d globally is actually in higher tid? No: if lower tid has many points with distance < d filling k slots, then sequential would also not include any distance-d point from lower tid (since they are > current worst). But when it reaches higher tid's point with distance d, its list still full with distances < d, so it won't include either. So no issue.\n\nThus merge by selecting min dist and tie-break by tid works.\n\nBut we must ensure that each thread's local top-k is exactly the same as sequential would produce for its chunk when considering only points in that chunk? Yes because insertion logic identical to sequential but restricted to subset; since order within chunk matches global order (contiguous), it yields correct partial result. Merging these sorted lists by distance and tid tie-break should yield global top-k.\n\nLet's